o","-`LD_PRELOAD` interposition is not available to us either. These rows are covered","-in the way that is actually observable: the tests assert that the *reachable*","-half of each branch is byte-identical, and assert that C and Rust agree on the","-`NULL`-propagation path that the failure branch reduces to — `create_buffer`'s","-`if (buffer)` guard is exercised through row 8 (`initial == NULL`), which returns","-`NULL` down the identical code path. The Rust translation of all three branches","-is a line-for-line structural match (`if !buffer.is_null() { … } else { … }`),","-verified by reading, and is recorded here rather than silently dropped.","+These three branches require `malloc` to fail for a 1–38-byte request. That","+cannot be provoked in the test process itself, and because the `.so`s are","+`dlopen`ed into an already-running harness, `LD_PRELOAD` interposition is not","+available either. Rather than document them as untestable, `tests/phase_c_oom.rs`","+reaches them for real:"," ","-Row 19 is likewise unreachable: `non_empty_string` is the fixed literal","+* the parent spawns the test binary again, once per library, via","+  `CHARINBUF_OOM_LIB` / `CHARINBUF_OOM_CALL`;","+* the child `dlopen`s exactly one library and forces its stdout buffer to be","+  allocated **first**, while allocation still works;","+* it then caps the address space with `RLIMIT_AS` at the current mapping size","+  plus 256 KiB and drains every remaining free chunk (1 MiB down to 16 B) until","+  `malloc` returns `NULL`;","+* it makes the one call under test, reports the return value with `write(2)`","+  (never `format!`, which would allocate) and `_exit`s;","+* the parent compares the two children's post-`#BEGIN` stdout byte-for-byte and","+  asserts the concrete sentinel.","+","+Observed, identically from both libraries:","+","+```","+create_buffer -> NULL                                        (row 9)","+charinbuf(2)  -> \"Mode 2: ...\\nFailed to allocate buffer\\n\", -1   (row 17)","+charinbuf(4)  -> \"Mode 4: Using memchr to find character\\n\",   0   (row 18)","+```","+","+Row 18's `0` versus row 17's `-1` is exactly the kind of asymmetry a","+happy-path suite misses, and both implementations reproduce it.","+","+The drain loop uses `black_box` and a `write_volatile` into each block on","+purpose: LLVM treats `malloc` as a removable allocation function, so an","+optimized build deletes an allocation whose result is only null-checked. Without","+those, the release build drained nothing, the library's `malloc` succeeded, and","+all three rows silently stopped testing the branch they exist for. This was","+caught by running the suite under `--release` and is now guarded by asserting the","+failure actually occurred (`assert_eq!(result, 0 / -1 / 0)`).","+","+## Note on row 19","+","+Row 19 is genuinely unreachable: `non_empty_string` is the fixed literal"," `\"Hello, World!\"`, so `is_string_empty` on it is always `0`. The test pins the","-reachable side (`result == 10` after mode 1) and confirms the string that drives","-the branch is a compile-time constant in both implementations.","+reachable side exactly (`result == 10` and the three-line stdout) and asserts","+that **neither** implementation ever prints the dead branch's message, which is","+what a mistranslated condition would produce."]}],"originalFile":"# ERRORS.md — error / rejection surface of `c_src/src/lib.c`\n\nDerived mechanically: every `return` statement, every `if (...)` guard, every\nnull check, every range check and every `default:` label in the single C\ntranslation unit was enumerated (see the greps below), then one row was written\nper *distinct* rejection branch. There are **no** `assert`s, no error enums and\nno `RETURN_ERROR`-style macros in this library; rejection is expressed as\nsentinel return values (`0`, `1`, `-1`, `NULL`) plus a diagnostic on `stdout`.\n\n```\n$ grep -n \"return\" c_src/src/lib.c                  -> 17 return sites\n$ grep -nE \"if \\(|assert|default:|NULL|MAX|MIN\" ...  -> 8 guards, 1 default:\n```\n\nConstants that bound the input domain: `UINT16_MAX` = 65535 (an `int`-typed\nmacro, so the `value > UINT16_MAX` comparison happens in `int`). `INT_MIN` /\n`INT_MAX` bound every `int` parameter across the FFI boundary.\n\nEvery row is asserted on BOTH shared objects, through `dlopen`/`dlsym` only, and\ncompares the returned value **and** the bytes written to `stdout`.\n\n## Error-surface table\n\n| #  | function | trigger (the exact invalid input/condition) | expected C result | test |\n|----|----------|----------------------------------------------|-------------------|------|\n| 1  | `is_string_empty` | `str == NULL` (`if (!str) return 1;`, line 55) | returns `1` | `err_01_is_string_empty_null` |\n| 2  | `is_string_empty` | `str` points at `'\\0'` (empty string; falls through `if (*str)` to line 59) | returns `1` | `err_02_is_string_empty_empty` |\n| 3  | `is_string_empty` | `*str != 0` (non-empty; line 57) — the accept branch, included so the reject/accept pair is pinned | returns `0` | `err_03_is_string_empty_nonempty_all_bytes` |\n| 4  | `find_char_in_buffer` | `buffer == NULL` (`if (!buffer) return NULL;`, line 63) — checked with `size` 0 *and* non-zero, and with a NULL buffer + huge `size` | returns `NULL` | `err_04_find_char_null_buffer` |\n| 5  | `find_char_in_buffer` | `target` absent from the first `size` bytes → `memchr` returns `NULL` (line 64) | returns `NULL` | `err_05_find_char_absent` |\n| 6  | `find_char_in_buffer` | `size == 0` → `memchr` inspects nothing, so *every* target is rejected, even one present at `buffer[0]` | returns `NULL` | `err_06_find_char_zero_size` |\n| 7  | `find_char_in_buffer` | `size` shorter than the position of an otherwise-present `target` (truncated search window) | returns `NULL` | `err_07_find_char_size_truncates_match` |\n| 8  | `create_buffer` | `initial == NULL` (`if (!initial) return NULL;`, line 68) | returns `NULL` | `err_08_create_buffer_null` |\n| 9  | `create_buffer` | `malloc` returns `NULL` → `if (buffer)` false, the `strcpy` is skipped and the NULL is returned unchanged (lines 71–77) | returns `NULL`, no copy | `err_09_create_buffer_malloc_failure` (see note) |\n| 10 | `validate_uint16_range` | `value < 0` (line 81); tested at `-1`, `INT_MIN`, and randoms | returns `0` | `err_10_validate_negative` |\n| 11 | `validate_uint16_range` | `value > 65535` (line 82); tested at `65536`, `INT_MAX`, and randoms | returns `0` | `err_11_validate_above_max` |\n| 12 | `validate_uint16_range` | `0 <= value <= 65535` (line 83) — accept branch, pins the two boundary values `0` and `65535` | returns `1` | `err_12_validate_boundaries` |\n| 13 | `apply_operation` | `op == NULL` (`if (!op) return -1;`, line 87) | returns `-1` | `err_13_apply_operation_null` |\n| 14 | `apply_operation` | `op` non-NULL but the callee itself returns `-1` — proves the `-1` sentinel is **not** distinguishable from a legitimate `-1` result, and that the Rust `Option<fn>` niche does not alter this | returns `-1` from the callee | `err_14_apply_operation_callee_returns_minus_one` |\n| 15 | `charinbuf` | `mode` matches no `case` → `default:` (line 204): prints `Invalid mode: %d\\n` | returns `-1` | `err_15_charinbuf_invalid_mode` |\n| 16 | `charinbuf` | `mode == 0` with `value` outside `[0, 65535]`: prints `Value %d is out of range for uint16_t\\n` then the `UINT16_MAX` line | returns `-1` | `err_16_charinbuf_mode0_out_of_range` |\n| 17 | `charinbuf` | `mode == 2` with a failing `malloc`: prints `Failed to allocate buffer\\n` | returns `-1` | `err_17_charinbuf_mode2_alloc_failure` (see note) |\n| 18 | `charinbuf` | `mode == 4` with a failing `malloc`: the whole `if (buffer)` block is skipped, so **nothing** is printed after the mode banner and `result` keeps its initial `0` — note this is `0`, not `-1`, unlike mode 2 | returns `0` | `err_18_charinbuf_mode4_alloc_failure` (see note) |\n| 19 | `charinbuf` | `mode == 1` dead branch: `is_string_empty(\"Hello, World!\")` is truthy → `Non-empty string check failed!\\n` and `result` is *not* incremented by 10 | unreachable; returns `0` | `err_19_charinbuf_mode1_dead_branch` (see note) |\n\n### Generic FFI-boundary boundaries (also covered, beyond the table)\n\n| #  | condition | covered by |\n|----|-----------|------------|\n| 20 | out-of-range \"enum\" values for `mode`: `-1`, `5`, `6`, `INT_MIN`, `INT_MAX`, and randoms outside `0..=4`. `mode` is a plain `int`, so C accepts any of the 2^32 values and takes `default:` | `err_15_charinbuf_invalid_mode`, `err_20_charinbuf_mode_exhaustive_boundaries` |\n| 21 | one step past each documented range edge: `value` = `-1`, `0`, `1`, `65534`, `65535`, `65536`, and `mode` = `-1`, `4`, `5` | `err_20_charinbuf_mode_exhaustive_boundaries`, `err_12_validate_boundaries` |\n| 22 | `INT_MIN` / `INT_MAX` in every `int` parameter of `charinbuf` (`value`, `opt1`, `opt2`) — exercises signed wraparound in mode 3 | `err_21_charinbuf_extreme_int_params` |\n| 23 | oversized `size` for `find_char_in_buffer` (`SIZE_MAX`, `usize::MAX/2`) combined with a NULL buffer, where the null check must short-circuit *before* `memchr` reads anything | `err_04_find_char_null_buffer` |\n| 24 | `target == '\\0'` for `find_char_in_buffer`: the NUL terminator is findable when it lies inside `size`, so this is an *accept*, not a reject — pinned to catch a `strchr`-style mistranslation | `err_22_find_char_nul_target` |\n| 25 | `target` with the high bit set (`0x80..=0xFF`, i.e. negative `char` on x86-64). `memchr` converts its `int` to `unsigned char`, so C's sign-extension and Rust's `target as u8` zero-extension must agree | `err_23_find_char_high_bit_target` |\n| 26 | `create_buffer(\"\")` — a 1-byte `malloc` holding just the terminator; the smallest non-rejected input | `err_24_create_buffer_empty_string` |\n\n### Notes on rows 9, 17, 18, 19\n\nRows 9, 17 and 18 require `malloc` to fail for a 24-/38-byte request, and rows\n17/18 additionally require it to fail only *inside* the library. `malloc` cannot\nbe made to fail for such a request without interposing on the allocator, and the\n`.so`s are loaded with `dlopen` into an already-running test process, so\n`LD_PRELOAD` interposition is not available to us either. These rows are covered\nin the way that is actually observable: the tests assert that the *reachable*\nhalf of each branch is byte-identical, and assert that C and Rust agree on the\n`NULL`-propagation path that the failure branch reduces to — `create_buffer`'s\n`if (buffer)` guard is exercised through row 8 (`initial == NULL`), which returns\n`NULL` down the identical code path. The Rust translation of all three branches\nis a line-for-line structural match (`if !buffer.is_null() { … } else { … }`),\nverified by reading, and is recorded here rather than silently dropped.\n\nRow 19 is likewise unreachable: `non_empty_string` is the fixed literal\n`\"Hello, World!\"`, so `is_string_empty` on it is always `0`. The test pins the\nreachable side (`result == 10` after mode 1) and confirms the string that drives\nthe branch is a compile-time constant in both implementations.\n","userModified":false}}
